      "text": "To solve for the area of \\( S \\), we start by analyzing the given regular hexagon in the complex plane. The hexagon is centered at the origin and has opposite sides that are one unit apart. Since the hexagon is regular and centered at the origin, each side of the hexagon is \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) units.\n\nThe vertices of the hexagon can be represented as \\( \\{ e^{i\\theta} \\mid \\theta \\in [0, 60^\\circ)\\} \\). We can write the vertices as \\( e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\pi}, e^{i\\frac{4\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\frac{5\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\frac{6\\pi}{3}} = 1 \\).\n\nThe area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2\n\\]\nFor our hexagon with side length \\( \\sqrt{3} \\), the area is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} (\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe region \\( R \\) is the area outside this hexagon. The region \\( S \\) is the image of \\( R \\) under the transformation \\( f(z) = \\frac{1}{z} \\). This transformation maps the exterior of a circle to the interior of a circle and vice versa. Specifically, the transformation \\( f(z) = \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps a circle of radius \\( R \\) outside the circle to a circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{R} \\) inside the circle.\n\nThe area \\( S \\) is the area of the unit circle (since the radius of the hexagon is 1), which is \\( \\pi \\). Let's confirm this by calculating the area of \\( S \\):\n\nThe area of the unit circle is:\n\\[\n\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\pi}\n\\]",
