      "text": "To find the area of the region \\( S \\), we start by understanding the geometry of the hexagon. A regular hexagon inscribed in the unit circle (centered at the origin) with opposite sides one unit apart means each side of the hexagon is \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) units long. One pair of sides is parallel to the imaginary axis.\n\nThe vertices of the hexagon can be represented as the 6th roots of unity scaled by \\( \\sqrt{3} \\). The 6th roots of unity are \\( 1, e^{i\\frac{\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\frac{2\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\pi}, e^{i\\frac{4\\pi}{3}}, e^{i\\frac{5\\pi}{3}} \\). Scaling these by \\( \\sqrt{3} \\), the vertices of the hexagon are:\n\\[ \\sqrt{3}, \\ \\sqrt{3}e^{\\frac{i\\pi}{3}}, \\ \\sqrt{3}e^{\\frac{2i\\pi}{3}}, \\ 1, \\ \\sqrt{3}e^{\\frac{4i\\pi}{3}}, \\ \\sqrt{3}e^{\\frac{5i\\pi}{3}}. \\]\n\nThe region \\( R \\) is the exterior of this hexagon. The transformation \\( z \\to \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the exterior of the hexagon to the interior of a circle centered at the origin with radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\). This is because if a point \\( z \\) lies outside the hexagon, then \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\) lies inside the circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\).\n\nThe area of the region outside the hexagon and inside the circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\) is the area of the unit circle minus the area of the hexagon. The area of the unit circle is \\( \\pi \\), and the area of the hexagon is the area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( \\sqrt{3} \\). The area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\) is given by \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2 \\). Here, \\( s = \\sqrt{3} \\), so the area of the hexagon is:\n\\[ \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} (\\sqrt{3})^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[ \\pi - \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\pi - \\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}}. \\]",
