      "text": "To find the area of \\( S = \\left\\lbrace\\frac{1}{z} \\left| z \\in R\\right\\rbrace \\) where \\( R \\) is the region outside a regular hexagon with center at the origin in the complex plane, we need to understand the transformation of the region under the map \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the properties of the hexagon. The hexagon is regular with center at the origin and opposite sides one unit apart. Since the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, the distance from the center to any vertex is the radius of the circle. Let's denote the radius of the circle by \\( R \\).\n\nThe vertices of the hexagon in the complex plane can be represented as the 6-th roots of unity scaled by \\( R \\). The vertices are \\( R \\text{cis} \\left( \\frac{k\\pi}{3} \\right) \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \\), where \\( \\text{cis} \\theta = \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta \\).\n\nNow, consider the transformation \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\). If \\( z = R \\text{cis} \\theta \\), then \\( w = \\frac{1}{R \\text{cis} \\theta} = \\frac{1}{R} \\text{cis} \\left( -\\theta \\right) \\). This means that \\( w \\) will be on the unit circle, but scaled by \\( \\frac{1}{R} \\). The transformation will map the hexagon to a region on the unit circle that is a scaled version of the hexagon.\n\nThe area of the region outside the hexagon inside the unit circle (i.e., the region \\( w \\) in \\( S \\)) is equal to the area of the hexagon multiplied by the square of the scaling factor \\( \\frac{1}{R} \\). The area of a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\) is given by \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2 \\). For our hexagon, the side length is one unit (since the distance from the center to any vertex is \\( R \\)), so the area of the hexagon is \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\nThe area of the region \\( S \\) is then:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } S = \\left( \\frac{1}{R} \\right)^2 \\times \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2R^2}\n\\]\n\nHowever, since the problem does not specify \\( R \\), and the transformation is a conformal map that preserves angles and area, the area of the region \\( S \\) is simply the area of the infinity minus the area of the hexagon inside the unit circle, which simplifies to the area of the unit circle minus the area of the hexagon. The area of the unit circle is \\( \\pi \\), and the area of the hexagon is \\( \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\), so the area of the region \\( S \\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of } S = \\pi - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2} \\pi - \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
